WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.190
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.846 --> 00:00:15.710
Hi, I'm Wil Torres, Assistant Director
with Stanford GSB's MBA Admissions Office.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.070
Today, we're joined by three students,
Sara, Jack and Marisabel,

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.718
who will share their experience
with the financial aid process.

00:00:21.718 --> 00:00:23.248
Sara, Jack,

00:00:23.248 --> 00:00:27.830
Marisabel, can you start off by telling
us a little bit about yourselves?

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.894
So let's start with where you're from and

00:00:29.894 --> 00:00:32.140
what you were doing before
you came to the GSB.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.360
&gt;&gt; So I was from Los Angeles and
running a charter school,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.270
kindergarten through eighth grade and a
team of people who helped empower parents

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:46.208
of charter school kids from
disadvantaged communities in L.A.

00:00:46.208 --> 00:00:46.750
&gt;&gt; Yep, so

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:50.168
I'm Jack,
I was in London before coming to the GSB.

00:00:50.168 --> 00:00:53.846
I worked in turn around and structuring as
a consultant there and moved to the US for

00:00:53.846 --> 00:00:55.510
the first time for business school.

00:00:57.034 --> 00:01:01.090
&gt;&gt; I'm Marisabel, I was in
San Francisco right before the GSB,

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.275
grew up in Southern California.

00:01:03.275 --> 00:01:07.892
Before business school, I was working in
tech on e-commerce and product management.

00:01:07.892 --> 00:01:11.924
&gt;&gt; All right, so
let's talk about your journeys to the GSB.

00:01:11.924 --> 00:01:16.742
Many prospects wonder if the opportunity
cost of taking time out from work to

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:21.450
pursue business degree is worth
the return on investment.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.350
How did you come to the decision that
it was the right time for you and and

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:26.430
that it was worth it?

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.015
&gt;&gt; So I can go first.

00:01:28.015 --> 00:01:31.461
I think for me, when I was
considering the opportunity cost,

00:01:31.461 --> 00:01:35.438
I think I had a really interesting
conversation with a colleague of mine

00:01:35.438 --> 00:01:39.102
who basically talked through it
being a marathon, not a sprint.

00:01:39.102 --> 00:01:41.319
And I felt like I was trying
to sprint through work.

00:01:41.319 --> 00:01:43.931
So I think that taking
the two years out to stop and

00:01:43.931 --> 00:01:46.885
reflect was the first thing
that I really wanted to do.

00:01:46.885 --> 00:01:50.987
And then I started to look into it and
did a lot of research online and

00:01:50.987 --> 00:01:55.823
found a lot of useful articles that talked
about the kind off payoff periods and

00:01:55.823 --> 00:01:58.698
the kind of transformative
experience it is.

00:01:58.698 --> 00:02:01.639
So for me I was thinking about,
is the experience going to be worth it?

00:02:01.639 --> 00:02:03.592
And then I was sort of
kind of running the numbers.

00:02:03.592 --> 00:02:04.755
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:02:04.755 --> 00:02:10.052
&gt;&gt; Similarly to Jack, I spoke to quite a
few different alumni, especially a handful

00:02:10.052 --> 00:02:15.298
from the GSB, to talk about what their
experiences were paying off their loans.

00:02:15.298 --> 00:02:18.351
because I really just wanted to
understand what that process looked,

00:02:18.351 --> 00:02:20.860
like having never taken out loans before.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.823
And so they helped me actually come
up with a model to look at different

00:02:25.823 --> 00:02:30.542
pay off periods given conservative or
different salary ranges.

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:34.312
And for me,
it seemed very worth it and feasible.

00:02:34.312 --> 00:02:37.235
And I also think less so
on the hard financial side but

00:02:37.235 --> 00:02:40.556
more about looking at these two
years is a very efficient way

00:02:40.556 --> 00:02:43.995
to understand what interests me and
what I wanted to pursue.

00:02:43.995 --> 00:02:47.058
And that I just deemed very invaluable.

00:02:47.058 --> 00:02:47.690
because I feel,

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:51.554
oftentimes when you're good at
something outside of business school or

00:02:51.554 --> 00:02:55.877
in your professional life, you will keep
doing that because you're good at it.

00:02:55.877 --> 00:02:58.991
But that doesn't necessarily mean that
that's what you want to be pursuing for

00:02:58.991 --> 00:03:00.700
the rest of your life.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.320
So I took both of those into
account in my decision to

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.900
to see that the investment was worth it.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.787
&gt;&gt; Before I started my research,
I assumed it was just too late for

00:03:10.787 --> 00:03:12.091
me to go business school.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:16.910
I was nearing the end of my 30s and
relatively happy in my career,

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:18.900
it had always been a dream.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:25.425
And a couple of conversations

00:03:25.425 --> 00:03:28.818
with Alumni and

00:03:28.818 --> 00:03:34.299
my executive coach led me

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:39.519
to realize that this one

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:45.783
year in the MSX program would

00:03:45.783 --> 00:03:51.800
give me a chance to step up.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.865
Probably wouldn't have the opportunity
to go in the future, and

00:03:54.865 --> 00:03:56.269
it was a great time to do that.

00:03:56.269 --> 00:04:00.780
&gt;&gt; Yeah, did you have any big
financial concerns coming into

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.382
the process of applying for
business school?

00:04:04.382 --> 00:04:08.230
And how did you address
some of those concerns?

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.905
&gt;&gt; Yes, [LAUGH] I'll start.

00:04:10.905 --> 00:04:15.337
I was certain I couldn't afford to
go to business school coming from

00:04:15.337 --> 00:04:17.213
a background in nonprofit.

00:04:17.213 --> 00:04:22.816
And looking at a few of the former
students who've gone here,

00:04:22.816 --> 00:04:27.451
the alumni also who pursued
their MBAs elsewhere,

00:04:27.451 --> 00:04:31.886
I just couldn't see how
the math would work out.

00:04:31.886 --> 00:04:37.242
Until I learned about the loan

00:04:37.242 --> 00:04:43.886
forgiveness program at Stanford and

00:04:43.886 --> 00:04:50.529
was blown away by the flexibility and

00:04:50.529 --> 00:04:53.315
the ability for

00:04:53.315 --> 00:04:58.670
me to take advantage of that,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:03.598
were I to continue to pursue

00:05:03.598 --> 00:05:08.118
a career in a nonprofit.

00:05:08.118 --> 00:05:11.290
&gt;&gt; A little bit more on
the loan forgiveness program.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:14.930
Can you tell us a little bit about that,
and what gave you the confidence that

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:18.820
this was something that could help
support your career aspirations?

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.770
&gt;&gt; Well, when you're looking at programs,
aside from Stanford,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.050
this program just doesn't exist.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:28.336
And that was something I hadn't
really realized that at Stanford,

00:05:28.336 --> 00:05:31.344
outside of Stanford,
I could take advantage of

00:05:31.344 --> 00:05:35.795
the federal loan forgiveness program,
which is super restrictive.

00:05:35.795 --> 00:05:40.682
It requires ten full years,
it has all these kind of catches to it,

00:05:40.682 --> 00:05:42.960
the technical catches.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.614
And few people are actually able
to take advantage of that program.

00:05:47.614 --> 00:05:51.713
So when I learned more about that program,
I was so sad,

00:05:51.713 --> 00:05:56.613
because I figured, gosh,
I really can't afford business school.

00:05:56.613 --> 00:06:00.344
And then when I learned about Stanford,
their own loan forgiveness program,

00:06:00.344 --> 00:06:02.219
I realized, okay, this makes sense.

00:06:02.219 --> 00:06:06.767
For every six months,
I believe it's six months, increments or

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:11.729
a year increments, as long as you're
working in nonprofit position,

00:06:11.729 --> 00:06:15.468
part time or full time,
that portion of your salary.

00:06:15.468 --> 00:06:19.780
And there's a sliding scale as to what
your salary can be up to a certain point.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:24.056
Percentage of that will be, of your loans,
your tuition, your living expenses,

00:06:24.056 --> 00:06:26.635
all the loans you've taken
out will be forgiven.

00:06:26.635 --> 00:06:31.547
And that to me was just such
an incredible benefit to

00:06:31.547 --> 00:06:35.546
offsetting the cost that it would take for

00:06:35.546 --> 00:06:41.090
someone from nonprofit
background here at Stanford.

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:46.011
&gt;&gt; Great, and then were there any other
concerns that you all had as you were

00:06:46.011 --> 00:06:48.360
thinking about business school?

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:52.208
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think for me, working in tech,

00:06:52.208 --> 00:06:56.738
if you invest enough time
at a certain company,

00:06:56.738 --> 00:07:01.394
there's a formula for
salary bumps and raises.

00:07:01.394 --> 00:07:07.906
So my concern was, are the two years
outside of the professional space,

00:07:07.906 --> 00:07:12.731
can I recoup that after
graduating business school?

00:07:12.731 --> 00:07:17.768
So I just took a hard look at the past
figures, numbers of the salaries that

00:07:17.768 --> 00:07:22.743
people earned right after business school,
especially Stanford GSB.

00:07:22.743 --> 00:07:27.702
And kind of weighed that out with what
I would be earning had I stayed in

00:07:27.702 --> 00:07:29.796
my role for these two years.

00:07:29.796 --> 00:07:34.015
And just saw that I would
still be better off.

00:07:34.015 --> 00:07:36.820
So that helped me a lot
with my decision as well.

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.863
Some other financial considerations,

00:07:40.863 --> 00:07:45.757
I have been helping to support my
family also, leading up to this point.

00:07:45.757 --> 00:07:49.031
So that was something that I
was very nervous about as well,

00:07:49.031 --> 00:07:53.140
just how would I be able to do
that during business school?

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:56.620
But with the financial aid
that Stanford gave me,

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:58.720
that relieved a bunch of the pressure.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:04.147
So both of those kind of just made it seem
much more feasible for me to still borrow

00:08:04.147 --> 00:08:09.826
the money and then have enough money to be
able to support my family while in school.

00:08:09.826 --> 00:08:12.158
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I definitely had
a lot of concerns coming in.

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:17.340
It was kind of my biggest worry about
deciding to come and do an MBA.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:22.130
I was convinced that the experience would
be great, but whether I could afford it or

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:23.507
not was the challenge.

00:08:23.507 --> 00:08:27.365
I think I spent a lot of times speaking
to the financial aid office ,who were

00:08:27.365 --> 00:08:31.529
fantastic and gave me loads of support,
pointed me in all the right directions in

00:08:31.529 --> 00:08:35.263
terms of thinking about where to get
loans as an international student,

00:08:35.263 --> 00:08:37.529
what kind of conversations to be having,
and

00:08:37.529 --> 00:08:42.110
then even just down to how much your
kind of daily costs are likely to be.

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:46.985
I think as well just the ease of
access to loans gave me confidence

00:08:46.985 --> 00:08:51.519
that they had confidence that
I'd be able to pay them back.

00:08:51.519 --> 00:08:54.207
So if it wasn't likely
they'll be able to afford it,

00:08:54.207 --> 00:08:57.389
they wouldn't be lending me
the money as easily as they could.

00:08:57.389 --> 00:09:01.030
Which I don't know if that makes sense,
but it did to me at the time.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.150
&gt;&gt; So I actually did receive
a full ride at another business

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:33.970
school through their scholarship program.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:38.843
Ultimately I decided to come to
the GSB because the financial aid was

00:09:38.843 --> 00:09:40.479
still very generous.

00:09:40.479 --> 00:09:43.853
But then also in working
with financial aid office,

00:09:43.853 --> 00:09:48.607
they brought to light some of these
extra scholarship opportunities that

00:09:48.607 --> 00:09:52.920
would be made available to me to apply to,
much like Sara covered.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.320
And at the end of day, what I want
to pursue after business school I

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:01.563
felt like Stanford GSB could set
me up in the best way possible.

00:10:01.563 --> 00:10:06.422
So I wasn't trying to optimize for
a completely free experience.

00:10:06.422 --> 00:10:11.272
But I was trying to optimize for
something that would have

00:10:11.272 --> 00:10:15.829
the best financial but
also personal ROI for myself.

00:10:15.829 --> 00:10:22.025
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I had some financial aid
from another school that I applied to.

00:10:22.025 --> 00:10:24.378
And I think the deciding factor for

00:10:24.378 --> 00:10:29.501
me was the generous amount that Stanford
financial aid package included.

00:10:29.501 --> 00:10:33.896
And also, in the grand scheme of things,
I wanted the kind of best experience and

00:10:33.896 --> 00:10:36.590
the most transformative experience.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.230
And for me, that was always the GSB.

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:41.670
So even if the numbers were slightly
different, I think it was more about

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.360
if I'm going to make the investment,
it's making the right investment.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:47.750
&gt;&gt; As you think about your
everyday experience here,

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:52.210
are there ways that you're able to be
thoughtful about either finances or

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:55.920
spending that still allow you
to maximize your time here?

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:03.310
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say that it's definitely
a conversation that people are having

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:07.170
more on campus now to create
a much more inclusive environment,

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:11.390
especially when it comes to clubs,
club dues, and those kinds of experiences.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:17.478
You can raise a concern, and I've heard
that the financial aid office and

00:11:17.478 --> 00:11:22.724
the student activities office have been
super helpful and receptive to that.

00:11:22.724 --> 00:11:26.853
And I do think that there's so many
extracurricular activities, there's so

00:11:26.853 --> 00:11:30.680
many options for how you want to
approach extracurricular activities.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:35.036
So if you want to spend a little more on
this one trip or this one event you can.

00:11:35.036 --> 00:11:39.441
But then there's so many other options
that you can also take advantage of and

00:11:39.441 --> 00:11:41.770
still have a great time while at the GSB.

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:44.610
So I would say that there are range of,
I guess,

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:48.661
costs associated with all
the extracurriculars that you want to do,

00:11:48.661 --> 00:11:52.377
so that we can create a much
more inclusive environment here.

00:11:52.377 --> 00:11:54.562
&gt;&gt; Yeah, all right.

00:11:54.562 --> 00:11:57.371
And as you're looking
back on your experience,

00:11:57.371 --> 00:12:01.958
how would you advise prospective students
as they think about financial aid and

00:12:01.958 --> 00:12:03.820
planning for business school?

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:10.665
&gt;&gt; I think the big thing for
me is that when it comes to planning,

00:12:10.665 --> 00:12:16.407
is think about trying to give yourself
a bit of a buffer coming in, if you can.

00:12:16.407 --> 00:12:21.028
I think you can take the loans, and
we talked about the financial aid and

00:12:21.028 --> 00:12:22.722
how generous that is, and

00:12:22.722 --> 00:12:28.190
you can run the numbers on the average
salary data post GSB and you'll be okay.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:32.097
But I think my biggest challenge
was the initial cash flow,

00:12:32.097 --> 00:12:37.111
which sounds a bit business schooly,
but just the flow of some of the funds.

00:12:37.111 --> 00:12:40.132
So if you can find yourself
a little bit of a buffer coming in,

00:12:40.132 --> 00:12:43.978
I think that would be my kind of advice or
tips in terms of how to think about it,

00:12:43.978 --> 00:12:45.777
easing some of the stress early on.

00:12:45.777 --> 00:12:49.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say that's exactly right,
especially in my case.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:53.533
Having never taken out loans before,
and I tried to be a good budgeter

00:12:53.533 --> 00:12:58.279
before business school, but maybe not
as reigned in as I would've liked.

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:03.360
Just looking at what your costs
were your first quarter here,

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:07.650
and just being realistic with yourself,
so not being too limiting or restrictive.

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:11.550
But just really understanding, okay, what
are the costs or how much am I going to

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:15.510
need to be able to support myself while
in business school this time, and

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:20.000
still give myself a little wiggle
room to enjoy my time here as well?

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.749
So I think at first I was very
restrictive, and that would stress me out.

00:13:25.749 --> 00:13:29.380
But then,
once I would look back at the models, or

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:33.530
whatever I had done before to
show that this was worth it,

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:38.646
I was able to come up with a budget
that helped me thrive here at the GSB.

00:13:38.646 --> 00:13:42.585
Yeah, in addition to having that
buffer before, planning for it after,

00:13:42.585 --> 00:13:46.968
especially if you're in the Sloan or MSX
program, given that it's a full year and

00:13:46.968 --> 00:13:49.273
we're fully engaged the year we are here.

00:13:49.273 --> 00:13:52.828
Most of us are spending our summers
transitioning into a job and

00:13:52.828 --> 00:13:54.512
don't have a job right away.

00:13:54.512 --> 00:13:58.196
And that requires to have that buffer,
so absolutely.

00:13:58.196 --> 00:14:03.897
I'd also suggest you just call
the financial aid office, talk to people,

00:14:03.897 --> 00:14:09.300
talk to alumni, and
people will be really, really supportive.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:14.077
There are some also financial planning
tools that you mentioned, sort of

00:14:14.077 --> 00:14:18.932
modeling out your salary and knowing
you have that confidence given the ROI

00:14:18.932 --> 00:14:24.276
you'll have based on your experience here,
that for me was incredibly helpful.

00:14:24.276 --> 00:14:27.368
&gt;&gt; All right, any final words of
encouragement you might have for

00:14:27.368 --> 00:14:28.890
applicants who are out there?

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:32.230
&gt;&gt; The GSB is so worth it.

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:34.820
Financial Aid will work with you.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:37.860
They'll come up with packages,
like Jack said,

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.740
that they're confident
will help you succeed.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:44.494
So I think it's such a good time
to just take a deep dive and

00:14:44.494 --> 00:14:49.282
enjoy your time here, and know that
you'll be supported along the way.

00:14:49.282 --> 00:14:49.922
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:14:49.922 --> 00:14:53.876
&gt;&gt; I'd also say it's an incredible
experience, so worth it.

00:14:53.876 --> 00:14:59.478
And when you get here, to pay
attention to the small opportunities

00:14:59.478 --> 00:15:03.990
to have an additional $20,000 scholarship.

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:08.974
There are these social innovation
certificates, for example,

00:15:08.974 --> 00:15:12.055
you can apply for and then be eligible for

00:15:12.055 --> 00:15:17.137
an additional grant that I wasn't
planning on and was able to get.

00:15:17.137 --> 00:15:22.224
So absolutely like to pay attention
to the emails and the opportunities

00:15:22.224 --> 00:15:26.818
to be a part of the financial aid
opportunities that exist here.

00:15:26.818 --> 00:15:30.305
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I just, sort of, if I could
interrupt by saying it's been a truly

00:15:30.305 --> 00:15:33.860
transformative experience so far and
I think there's only more to come.

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:37.504
And I'm kind of excited for the summer
to test out sort of what we're doing and

00:15:37.504 --> 00:15:40.440
get back to the real world for
a bit and come back for next year.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.792
So I think it's been worth every penny.

00:15:42.792 --> 00:15:46.290
&gt;&gt; All right, and so
that's a great point to close on.

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:47.650
Great multiple points.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:48.700
The time is worth it.

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:52.735
This is an investing in an experience and
your future career.

00:15:52.735 --> 00:15:55.826
You can learn more about both the MBA and

00:15:55.826 --> 00:16:00.988
the MSX program by visiting us
on the web at gsb.stanford.edu.

00:16:00.988 --> 00:16:03.628
Thank you, and have a great day.

00:16:03.628 --> 00:16:13.496
[MUSIC]

